(invariants.vox - the sameness detector.

 Takes a directory of generated .vox programs (produced by
 `vox-fuzz gen --keep <dir>`) and reports everything that does NOT vary
 across the corpus: identical statements, construct counts fixed at the
 same N in every program, fixed relative ordering between constructs,
 numeric literals that never exceed some bound, and identifiers spelled
 the same in every program (the signature of a hardcoded prelude).

 The unit of comparison is the STATEMENT, normalised - never the raw
 line. A generated program's layout is drawn, so the line is not where
 sameness lives; see "normalisation" below for what that means and for
 the measurement that forced it.

 The standard this enforces (see the brief): variation is the default:
 sameness is what needs a citation. This tool does not adjudicate which
 invariants are justified - it reports them all, ranked by how suspicious
 they are (100% presence outranks 80%), and leaves a citation column for
 a human to fill in.

 Vox has no directory-listing primitive (no readdir/glob), so file
 discovery shells out to `find`, exactly the way src/harness.vox already
 shells out for anything a single execve cannot do alone. Everything
 downstream - normalising, tokenizing, counting, sorting - is plain Vox:
 `Read line` (0.1.x+) reads the file listing back a line at a time, maps
 give O(1) frequency tables keyed by arbitrary text, and buffers give
 the byte-level scanning the normaliser, the classifier and the digit-run
 bounds check all run on.)

a number called 'suspicion threshold percent' is 50.
(300, not a small number, on purpose: for a 60-program run the raw
 finding count lands well under 1000, and every 100%-suspicion finding
 ties on score, so a small cap plus stable tie-breaking would silently
 drop entire categories - fixed-ordering and half of fixed-vocabulary
 vanished below an 80-item cap in testing. Aggregation still kicks in
 above this, so a truly noisy corpus does not flood the report.)
a number called 'max findings shown' is 300.

(accumulator maps: text key -> number. The key of the first two is a
 normalised STATEMENT - see the normaliser below - not a raw line.)
a map called program_count_of_statement is {}.
a map called total_count_of_statement is {}.
a map called program_count_of_ident is {}.
a map called skeleton_program_count is {}.
a map called skeleton_max_slot is {}.
a map called skeleton_slot_count is {}.

(cross-call scratch, written by helper functions as documented side
 effects - the same pattern src/harness.vox uses for 'the last status')
a list called 'the slot values' is [].
a list called 'the tokens' is [].

(the normaliser's scratch: the program being normalised, the statement
 being accumulated, the one-byte staging buffer every emission goes
 through - Vox appends buffers to buffers, so one byte is a one-byte
 buffer - and the finished statements of the program last normalised.)
Create a buffer called norm_src.
Create a buffer called norm_statement.
a buffer called norm_byte is 1 bytes in size.
a list called 'the statements' is [].

(corpus-wide accumulators)
a list called 'all program paths' is [].
a list called 'all program statements' is [].
a number called 'programs processed' is 0.

(min starts absurdly high and max absurdly low so the very first
 program's counts always win both comparisons - no "is this the first
 program" branch needed anywhere below)
a number called flags_min is 999999.
a number called flags_max is 0.
a number called things_min is 999999.
a number called things_max is 0.
a number called functions_min is 999999.
a number called functions_max is 0.
a number called globals_min is 999999.
a number called globals_max is 0.
a number called buffers_min is 999999.
a number called buffers_max is 0.
a number called loops_min is 999999.
a number called loops_max is 0.

(the ranked report, built as three parallel lists)
a list called 'report scores' is [].
a list called 'report categories' is [].
a list called 'report texts' is [].

(the 100%-present statements, used for the ordering / contiguity pass)
a list called 'invariant statement keys' is [].

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 small helpers
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

(shell out and wait - the one place this tool touches a process outside
 itself, for the one thing Vox cannot do alone: list a directory.)
To 'run shell' with a text called 'the command'.
    a list called 'the shell arguments' is [].
    append "-c" to 'the shell arguments'.
    append 'the command' to 'the shell arguments'.
    a number called 'the pid' is fork the process.
    If 'the pid' is 0 then, Execute "/bin/sh" with arguments 'the shell arguments', Exit 127.
    a number called 'the reaped' is reap process 'the pid'.
    Return a number, 'the reaped'.

(single-quote the whole value POSIX-style, so a corpus directory with
 spaces or quotes in its path cannot break the `find` command line.)
To 'shell quote' with a text called path.
    a buffer called 'the source bytes' is path.
    a buffer called 'the quoted bytes' is "'".
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the source bytes's size.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the source bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 39 then, append "'\\''" to 'the quoted bytes'.
        If 'the current byte' is not 39 then, Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the current byte', append 'one byte' to 'the quoted bytes'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    append "'" to 'the quoted bytes'.
    a text called result is 'the quoted bytes' as text.
    Return a text, result.

(does `the subject` begin with the literal text `prefix`? Used for
 construct classification - flags/things/functions/buffers/loops are all
 recognisable from a fixed spelling at the head of the statement.)
To 'starts with' with a buffer called 'the subject' and a text called prefix.
    a buffer called 'prefix bytes' is prefix.
    a number called 'prefix length' is 'prefix bytes's size.
    a boolean called result is true.
    If 'the subject's size is less than 'prefix length' then, Set result to false.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'prefix length' and result,
        a number called 'the subject byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the subject',
        a number called 'the prefix byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'prefix bytes',
        If 'the subject byte' is not 'the prefix byte' then, Set result to false.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    Return a boolean, result.

(is `token` one of Vox's own keywords, rather than a name the generator
 chose? Checked case-insensitively against a lowercase copy, since
 statement starters are capitalized and everything else generally is
 not. This list only needs to cover words that could plausibly appear
 as a bare alphabetic token inside generated source - see LANGUAGE.md's
 Keywords section.)
To 'lowercase copy' with a text called value.
    a buffer called 'source bytes' is value.
    a buffer called out.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'source bytes's size.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'source bytes',
        a number called 'lowercase byte' is 'the byte',
        If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 65 and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 90 then, Set 'lowercase byte' to 'the byte' add 32.
        Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'lowercase byte',
        append 'one byte' to out,
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    a text called result is out as text.
    Return a text, result.

To 'is keyword' with a text called token.
    a text called 'lowercase token' is 'lowercase copy' of token.
    a list called keywords is ["a", "an", "the", "print", "set", "create", "if", "when", "while", "for", "to", "return", "increment", "decrement", "break", "continue", "exit", "append", "change", "remove", "delete", "mount", "unmount", "umount", "pivot", "execute", "shutdown", "poweroff", "reboot", "restart", "halt", "fork", "reap", "send", "signal", "flag", "flags", "parse", "required", "default", "with", "called", "named", "of", "on", "and", "or", "but", "then", "otherwise", "else", "from", "is", "not", "equal", "greater", "less", "than", "each", "in", "element", "byte", "bytes", "number", "text", "boolean", "float", "buffer", "list", "map", "value", "thing", "has", "do", "function", "true", "false", "nothing", "empty", "size", "length", "capacity", "raw", "all", "first", "last", "second", "count", "type", "add", "subtract", "multiply", "divide", "modulo", "times", "absolute", "even", "odd", "positive", "negative", "zero", "arguments", "environment", "open", "close", "read", "write", "seek", "line", "standard", "input", "output", "error", "without", "waiting", "available", "as", "at", "into", "resize", "reallocate", "grow", "shrink", "copy", "clear", "keys", "values", "treating", "repeat", "times.", "current", "time", "wait", "or_equal", "start", "stop", "begin", "finish"].
    a number called 'the keyword count' is keywords's length.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a boolean called found is false.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the keyword count' and found is false,
        a text called 'candidate keyword' is element 'the scan position' of keywords,
        If 'lowercase token' is 'candidate keyword' then, Set found to true.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    Return a boolean, found.

(statement -> skeleton: every maximal run of ASCII digits becomes one '#',
 and the run's numeric value is pushed to the global 'the slot values',
 in left-to-right order. Two programs whose lines differ only in which
 literal or which generator-assigned numeric suffix they used collapse
 to the same skeleton, which is what lets the bounds check see through
 "b1"/"b2"/"b3" and "40"/"61" to the shared template underneath.)
To 'skeletonize' with a text called 'the statement text'.
    Set 'the slot values' to [].
    a buffer called 'source bytes' is 'the statement text'.
    a buffer called 'skeleton bytes'.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'source bytes's size.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a boolean called 'in run' is false.
    a number called 'run value' is 0.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'source bytes',
        a boolean called 'is digit' is false,
        If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 48 and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 57 then, Set 'is digit' to true.
        a boolean called 'start of run' is false,
        If 'is digit' and 'in run' is false then, Set 'start of run' to true.
        If 'start of run' then, Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to '#', append 'one byte' to 'skeleton bytes', Set 'in run' to true, Set 'run value' to 0.
        If 'is digit' then, Set 'run value' to {'run value' multiply 10} add {'the byte' subtract 48}.
        a boolean called 'end of run' is false,
        If 'is digit' is false and 'in run' then, Set 'end of run' to true.
        If 'end of run' then, append 'run value' to 'the slot values', Set 'in run' to false.
        If 'is digit' is false then, Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the byte', append 'one byte' to 'skeleton bytes'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    If 'in run' then, append 'run value' to 'the slot values'.
    a text called skeleton is 'skeleton bytes' as text.
    Return a text, skeleton.

(statement -> identifier tokens: maximal runs of [A-Za-z0-9_] whose
 first character is a letter. Pushed to the global 'the tokens', left to
 right; duplicates within one statement are fine, the caller dedupes.)
To 'tokenize identifiers' with a text called 'the statement text'.
    Set 'the tokens' to [].
    a buffer called 'source bytes' is 'the statement text'.
    a buffer called 'token bytes'.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'source bytes's size.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a boolean called 'in token' is false.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'source bytes',
        a boolean called 'is upper' is false,
        If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 65 and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 90 then, Set 'is upper' to true.
        a boolean called 'is lower' is false,
        If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 97 and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 122 then, Set 'is lower' to true.
        a boolean called 'is digit' is false,
        If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 48 and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 57 then, Set 'is digit' to true.
        a boolean called 'is underscore' is false,
        If 'the byte' is 95 then, Set 'is underscore' to true.
        a boolean called 'is letter' is false,
        If 'is upper' or 'is lower' then, Set 'is letter' to true.
        a boolean called 'is alnum' is false,
        If 'is letter' or 'is digit' or 'is underscore' then, Set 'is alnum' to true.
        a boolean called 'start of token' is false,
        If 'in token' is false and 'is letter' then, Set 'start of token' to true.
        If 'start of token' then, clear 'token bytes', Set 'in token' to true.
        If 'in token' and 'is alnum' then, Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the byte', append 'one byte' to 'token bytes'.
        a boolean called 'end of token' is false,
        If 'in token' and 'is alnum' is false then, Set 'end of token' to true.
        ('token bytes' is cleared and reused for every token in this
         statement, so a text taken straight off it would alias whatever
         it holds by the time the caller reads 'the tokens' back - the
         same "as text does not copy" hazard 'norm flush statement'
         documents. Copy through a fresh buffer per token instead.)
        a buffer called 'token bytes copy' is 'token bytes',
        If 'end of token' then, a text called finished is 'token bytes copy' as text, append finished to 'the tokens', Set 'in token' to false.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    a buffer called 'token bytes copy last' is 'token bytes'.
    If 'in token' then, a text called last is 'token bytes copy last' as text, append last to 'the tokens'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 normalisation: the unit of comparison is the STATEMENT, not the line
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------

 A generated program's layout is drawn - gen_core.vox's 'gen relayout'
 rewrites every whitespace run: the indent width, where the line breaks,
 how wide each gap is, and whether a gap beside punctuation is there at
 all. None of that changes what the program says. LANGUAGE.md:226 says
 Vox ignores leading whitespace and a program can be minified without
 changing its meaning, and periods, commas and paragraph breaks are the
 only flow control there is.

 So a detector that compares raw LINES reports a corpus laid out one
 statement per line as riddled with sameness and the SAME programs laid
 out at random as nearly clean - not because the sameness went away but
 because the line stopped being the unit it lives in. Measured on 150
 seeds from 5000, budget 12: 115 rows at 100% presence under
 --layout plain, 35 under the default random layout, on programs whose
 whitespace-stripped bytes are pairwise identical.

 Each program is therefore normalised before it is measured. The unit is
 the statement - LANGUAGE.md "Basics": a sentence ending in a period -
 and the normal form is:

   - a whitespace run becomes ONE space, or nothing at all when the byte
     on either side of it is punctuation the layout pass may close a gap
     beside: . , ( ) " [ ] { } : - the set is 'layout byte is
     punctuation' in gen_core.vox, and matching it exactly is what makes
     this normal form layout-INVARIANT rather than merely tidier. A gap
     the randomiser is allowed to delete must not survive here either.
   - a period ends the statement, unless it has a digit on both sides,
     where it is a decimal point instead: LANGUAGE.md:1910 and
     gen_literals.vox record that `3.` and `.5` are both parse errors,
     so "digits on both sides" is the whole rule. A run of periods
     closes several clauses at once and belongs to the statement it
     ends; whitespace inside such a run is stepped over, since the
     randomiser may delete it beside a period.
   - a string literal, a parenthesised comment and a quoted multi-word
     name are copied through untouched. The whitespace in them is data
     or part of a name, and the layout pass leaves them alone too, so
     the scanners below mirror 'layout copy string', 'layout copy
     comment', 'layout copy quoted name' and 'layout apostrophe opens a
     name' byte for byte. A comment ends no statement - it has no period
     of its own outside itself - so it stays attached to the statement
     that follows it, which also means a statement opening with one is
     not classified as a declaration. No generated program carries a
     comment today; whoever makes the generator emit them should decide
     then whether a comment is its own unit, and say why.

 Checked rather than assumed: normalising the 150-seed plain corpus and
 the random corpus of the SAME seeds gives the same statement list for
 all 150 programs, and the two reports agree row for row.)

To 'norm put byte' with a number called b.
    Set byte 1 of norm_byte to b.
    append norm_byte to norm_statement.

(Space 32, tab 9, newline 10, carriage return 13 - gen_core.vox's
 'layout byte is whitespace'.)
To 'norm byte is whitespace' with a number called b.
    If b is 32 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 9 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 10 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 13 then, Return a boolean, true.
    Return a boolean, false.

(The bytes a gap may shrink to nothing beside - gen_core.vox's 'layout
 byte is punctuation', kept identical to it on purpose. 0 stands in for
 "no neighbour" at either end of the file and matches nothing here.)
To 'norm byte is punctuation' with a number called b.
    If b is 46 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 44 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 40 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 41 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 34 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 91 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 93 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 123 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 125 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 58 then, Return a boolean, true.
    Return a boolean, false.

To 'norm byte is digit' with a number called b.
    If b is greater than or equal to 48 and b is less than or equal to 57 then, Return a boolean, true.
    Return a boolean, false.

(Letters, digits and underscore - what a name is made of. Used only to
 tell a possessive apostrophe from the one that opens a quoted name.)
To 'norm byte is name character' with a number called b.
    If b is greater than 96 and b is less than 123 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is greater than 64 and b is less than 91 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is greater than 47 and b is less than 58 then, Return a boolean, true.
    If b is 95 then, Return a boolean, true.
    Return a boolean, false.

(Copy a double-quoted string literal through untouched, from its opening
 quote to just past its closing one; a backslash escapes whatever
 follows, so an escaped quote does not close the string. Returns the
 position after the literal.)
To 'norm copy string' with a number called 'the opening position'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called 'the opening byte' is byte 'the opening position' of norm_src.
    'norm put byte' of 'the opening byte'.
    a number called i is 'the opening position' add 1.
    a boolean called 'the string is closed' is false.
    While 'the string is closed' is false and i is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte i of norm_src,
        'norm put byte' of b,
        a number called 'the stride' is 1,
        If b is 92 and i is less than n then, a number called 'the escaped position' is i add 1, a number called 'the escaped byte' is byte 'the escaped position' of norm_src, 'norm put byte' of 'the escaped byte', Set 'the stride' to 2.
        If b is 34 then, Set 'the string is closed' to true.
        Set i to i add 'the stride'.
    Return a number, i.

(Copy a parenthesised comment through untouched, nesting and all - so
 this does not stop at the first close paren, and the string scanner
 does not run inside it.)
To 'norm copy comment' with a number called 'the opening position'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called 'the nesting depth' is 0.
    a number called i is 'the opening position'.
    a boolean called 'the comment is closed' is false.
    While 'the comment is closed' is false and i is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte i of norm_src,
        'norm put byte' of b,
        If b is 40 then, Set 'the nesting depth' to 'the nesting depth' add 1.
        If b is 41 then, Set 'the nesting depth' to 'the nesting depth' minus 1.
        If b is 41 and 'the nesting depth' is 0 then, Set 'the comment is closed' to true.
        Set i to i add 1.
    Return a number, i.

(Copy a single-quoted name through untouched. The space in 'made at' is
 part of the name, not layout.)
To 'norm copy quoted name' with a number called 'the opening position'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called 'the opening byte' is byte 'the opening position' of norm_src.
    'norm put byte' of 'the opening byte'.
    a number called i is 'the opening position' add 1.
    a boolean called 'the name is closed' is false.
    While 'the name is closed' is false and i is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte i of norm_src,
        'norm put byte' of b,
        If b is 39 then, Set 'the name is closed' to true.
        Set i to i add 1.
    Return a number, i.

(An apostrophe is one of two things and the normaliser has to tell them
 apart: the possessive in arguments's count, which is ordinary source,
 or the opening quote of a multi-word name like 'made at', whose insides
 are untouchable. The tell - an s and then a byte that cannot continue a
 name is a possessive, anything else opens a name - is gen_core.vox's
 'layout apostrophe opens a name', and the doubt resolves the same way,
 toward "a name opens here": misreading a name as a possessive would
 normalise the whitespace INSIDE the name, and the layout pass, reading
 it the same way, never touched it.)
To 'norm apostrophe opens a name' with a number called 'the apostrophe position'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called 'the next position' is 'the apostrophe position' add 1.
    If 'the next position' is greater than n then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the next byte' is byte 'the next position' of norm_src.
    If 'the next byte' is not 115 then,
        Return a boolean, true.
    a number called 'the third position' is 'the apostrophe position' add 2.
    If 'the third position' is greater than n then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the third byte' is byte 'the third position' of norm_src.
    a boolean called 'the third continues a name' is 'norm byte is name character' of 'the third byte'.
    If 'the third continues a name' then,
        Return a boolean, true.
    Return a boolean, false.

(One whitespace run to its normal form: one space, or nothing beside
 punctuation or a file edge. Returns the position after the run.)
To 'norm rewrite whitespace run' with a number called 'the run start'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called i is 'the run start'.
    a boolean called scanning is true.
    While scanning and i is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte i of norm_src,
        a boolean called 'still whitespace' is 'norm byte is whitespace' of b,
        If 'still whitespace' is false then, Set scanning to false.
        If scanning then, Set i to i add 1.
    a number called 'the previous byte' is 0.
    If 'the run start' is greater than 1 then, a number called 'the previous position' is 'the run start' minus 1, Set 'the previous byte' to byte 'the previous position' of norm_src.
    a number called 'the next byte' is 0.
    If i is less than or equal to n then, Set 'the next byte' to byte i of norm_src.
    a boolean called 'a space survives' is true.
    If 'the previous byte' is 0 then, Set 'a space survives' to false.
    If 'the next byte' is 0 then, Set 'a space survives' to false.
    a boolean called 'the previous is punctuation' is 'norm byte is punctuation' of 'the previous byte'.
    If 'the previous is punctuation' then, Set 'a space survives' to false.
    a boolean called 'the next is punctuation' is 'norm byte is punctuation' of 'the next byte'.
    If 'the next is punctuation' then, Set 'a space survives' to false.
    If 'a space survives' then, 'norm put byte' of 32.
    Return a number, i.

(Is the period at this position a decimal point rather than the end of a
 statement? Digits on both sides and nothing else: LANGUAGE.md:1910 -
 `3.` and `.5` are both parse errors, hand-verified in gen_literals.vox,
 so a float always has a digit either side of its dot.)
To 'norm period is a decimal point' with a number called 'the period position'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    If 'the period position' is less than 2 then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the previous position' is 'the period position' minus 1.
    a number called 'the previous byte' is byte 'the previous position' of norm_src.
    a boolean called 'a digit precedes' is 'norm byte is digit' of 'the previous byte'.
    If 'a digit precedes' is false then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the next position' is 'the period position' add 1.
    If 'the next position' is greater than n then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the next byte' is byte 'the next position' of norm_src.
    a boolean called 'a digit follows' is 'norm byte is digit' of 'the next byte'.
    Return a boolean, 'a digit follows'.

(Take the whole terminator: the period that ends the statement plus any
 further periods closing outer clauses with it. Whitespace inside the
 run is stepped over rather than allowed to break it, because beside a
 period the randomiser may delete it. Only the periods are kept.
 Returns the position after the run.)
To 'norm take terminator run' with a number called 'the period position'.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called i is 'the period position'.
    a boolean called scanning is true.
    While scanning and i is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte i of norm_src,
        a boolean called 'is a period' is false,
        If b is 46 then, Set 'is a period' to true.
        a boolean called 'is blank' is 'norm byte is whitespace' of b,
        If 'is a period' then, 'norm put byte' of b.
        If 'is a period' is false and 'is blank' is false then, Set scanning to false.
        If scanning then, Set i to i add 1.
    Return a number, i.

(Close the statement being accumulated and push it, if it holds
 anything. `buffer as text` aliases the buffer's own storage rather than
 copying, and this buffer is cleared and reused for the next statement,
 so the text is taken off a fresh copy - the same hazard the token
 scanner documents below.)
To 'norm flush statement'.
    a number called 'the length' is norm_statement's size.
    a buffer called 'the statement copy' is norm_statement.
    If 'the length' is greater than 0 then, a text called 'the finished statement' is 'the statement copy' as text, append 'the finished statement' to 'the statements'.
    clear norm_statement.

(A whole program to its statements, left in the global 'the statements'.)
To 'split into statements' with a buffer called 'the program bytes'.
    clear norm_src.
    append 'the program bytes' to norm_src.
    Set 'the statements' to [].
    clear norm_statement.
    a number called n is norm_src's size.
    a number called i is 1.
    While i is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte i of norm_src,
        a number called 'the position after' is i add 1,
        a boolean called 'is whitespace' is 'norm byte is whitespace' of b,
        If 'is whitespace' then, a number called 'the run end' is 'norm rewrite whitespace run' of i, Set 'the position after' to 'the run end'.
        If b is 34 then, a number called 'the string end' is 'norm copy string' of i, Set 'the position after' to 'the string end'.
        If b is 40 then, a number called 'the comment end' is 'norm copy comment' of i, Set 'the position after' to 'the comment end'.
        a boolean called 'a name opens here' is false,
        If b is 39 then, Set 'a name opens here' to 'norm apostrophe opens a name' of i.
        If b is 39 and 'a name opens here' then, a number called 'the name end' is 'norm copy quoted name' of i, Set 'the position after' to 'the name end'.
        If b is 39 and 'a name opens here' is false then, 'norm put byte' of b.
        a boolean called 'is a period' is false,
        If b is 46 then, Set 'is a period' to true.
        a boolean called 'a decimal point' is false,
        If 'is a period' then, Set 'a decimal point' to 'norm period is a decimal point' of i.
        If 'is a period' and 'a decimal point' then, 'norm put byte' of b.
        If 'is a period' and 'a decimal point' is false then, a number called 'the terminator end' is 'norm take terminator run' of i, Set 'the position after' to 'the terminator end', 'norm flush statement'.
        a boolean called 'the byte stands alone' is true,
        If 'is whitespace' then, Set 'the byte stands alone' to false.
        If b is 34 then, Set 'the byte stands alone' to false.
        If b is 40 then, Set 'the byte stands alone' to false.
        If b is 39 then, Set 'the byte stands alone' to false.
        If 'is a period' then, Set 'the byte stands alone' to false.
        If 'the byte stands alone' then, 'norm put byte' of b.
        Set i to 'the position after'.
    (a final statement with no closing period - a truncated program -
     is still worth measuring, so whatever is left is pushed)
    'norm flush statement'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 per-program pass: read the file once, update every accumulator.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

To 'process program' with a text called path.
    (One Read fills a dynamic buffer with the whole file - hand-verified
     on an 8,824-byte generated program - so the normaliser sees the
     program as the byte stream it is, with no line structure imposed on
     it before the statements are found.)
    open a file for reading called 'the program file' at path.
    a buffer called 'the program bytes'.
    Read from 'the program file' into 'the program bytes'.
    Close 'the program file'.
    'split into statements' of 'the program bytes'.
    a list called 'program statements' is [].
    a map called 'seen statements' is {}.
    a map called 'seen skeletons' is {}.
    a map called 'seen idents' is {}.
    a number called 'flags count' is 0.
    a number called 'things count' is 0.
    a number called 'functions count' is 0.
    a number called 'declarations count' is 0.
    a number called 'buffers count' is 0.
    a number called 'loops count' is 0.
    a number called 'how many statements' is 'the statements's length.
    a number called si is 1.
    While si is less than or equal to 'how many statements',
        a text called 'this statement' is element si of 'the statements',
        (the classifier reads bytes, so the statement goes back through a
         buffer of its own - a fresh declaration from a text copies)
        a buffer called 'statement bytes' is 'this statement',
        append 'this statement' to 'program statements',
        (classify: which construct, if any, does this statement declare?
         precedence matters - "A thing called" and "a flag called" both
         start with the article that also opens a bare declaration.)
        a boolean called 'is flag' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "a flag called ",
        a boolean called 'is thing' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "A thing called ",
        a boolean called 'is function' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "To ",
        a boolean called 'is buffer' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "a buffer called ",
        a boolean called 'is loop' is false,
        a boolean called 'is while' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "While ",
        a boolean called 'is for' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "For each ",
        If 'is while' or 'is for' then, Set 'is loop' to true.
        a boolean called 'is bare a' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "a ",
        a boolean called 'is bare cap a' is 'starts with' of 'statement bytes' and "A ",
        a boolean called 'is bare either' is false,
        If 'is bare a' then, Set 'is bare either' to true.
        If 'is bare cap a' then, Set 'is bare either' to true.
        a boolean called 'is declaration' is false,
        If 'is flag' is false and 'is thing' is false and 'is buffer' is false and 'is bare either' then, Set 'is declaration' to true.
        If 'is flag' then, Set 'flags count' to 'flags count' add 1.
        If 'is thing' then, Set 'things count' to 'things count' add 1.
        If 'is function' then, Set 'functions count' to 'functions count' add 1.
        If 'is buffer' then, Set 'buffers count' to 'buffers count' add 1.
        If 'is loop' then, Set 'loops count' to 'loops count' add 1.
        If 'is declaration' then, Set 'declarations count' to 'declarations count' add 1.
        (identical-statement dedup: only the first occurrence in THIS
         program advances the corpus-wide program_count_of_statement map)
        a number called 'already this statement' is 'seen statements's "{'this statement'}",
        a number called 'statement pcount' is program_count_of_statement's "{'this statement'}",
        a number called 'statement pcount next' is 'statement pcount' add 1,
        If 'already this statement' is 0 then, Set program_count_of_statement's "{'this statement'}" to 'statement pcount next', Set 'seen statements's "{'this statement'}" to 1.
        a number called 'statement tcount' is total_count_of_statement's "{'this statement'}",
        a number called 'statement tcount next' is 'statement tcount' add 1,
        Set total_count_of_statement's "{'this statement'}" to 'statement tcount next',
        (skeleton / bounds)
        a text called skeleton is 'skeletonize' of 'this statement',
        a number called 'slot count' is 'the slot values's length,
        a boolean called 'has slots' is false,
        If 'slot count' is greater than 0 then, Set 'has slots' to true.
        If 'has slots' then, Set skeleton_slot_count's "{skeleton}" to 'slot count'.
        a number called 'already this skeleton' is 0,
        If 'has slots' then, Set 'already this skeleton' to 'seen skeletons's "{skeleton}".
        a number called 'skel pcount' is 0,
        If 'has slots' then, Set 'skel pcount' to skeleton_program_count's "{skeleton}".
        a number called 'skel pcount next' is 'skel pcount' add 1,
        If 'has slots' and 'already this skeleton' is 0 then, Set skeleton_program_count's "{skeleton}" to 'skel pcount next', Set 'seen skeletons's "{skeleton}" to 1.
        a number called sj is 1,
        While sj is less than or equal to 'slot count',
            a number called val is element sj of 'the slot values',
            a text called 'slot key' is "{skeleton}#{sj}",
            a number called cur is skeleton_max_slot's "{'slot key'}",
            If val is greater than cur then, Set skeleton_max_slot's "{'slot key'}" to val.
            Set sj to sj add 1.
        (fixed vocabulary: identifier tokens, keywords excluded)
        'tokenize identifiers' of 'this statement',
        a number called 'token count' is 'the tokens's length,
        a number called tj is 1,
        While tj is less than or equal to 'token count',
            a text called 'this token' is element tj of 'the tokens',
            a boolean called 'is a keyword' is 'is keyword' of 'this token',
            a boolean called 'not a keyword' is false,
            If 'is a keyword' is false then, Set 'not a keyword' to true.
            a number called 'already this ident' is 0,
            If 'not a keyword' then, Set 'already this ident' to 'seen idents's "{'this token'}".
            a number called 'ident pcount' is 0,
            If 'not a keyword' then, Set 'ident pcount' to program_count_of_ident's "{'this token'}".
            a number called 'ident pcount next' is 'ident pcount' add 1,
            If 'not a keyword' and 'already this ident' is 0 then, Set program_count_of_ident's "{'this token'}" to 'ident pcount next', Set 'seen idents's "{'this token'}" to 1.
            Set tj to tj add 1.
        (the loop's LAST statement must be a plain, unconditional,
         non-nested one - see the report for why: a bare `If` with no
         `Otherwise`, or a nested `While`, left as the last thing in a
         loop body does not close the loop's own clause, so the loop
         either stops after one pass or swallows the code after it.)
        Set si to si add 1.
    append 'program statements' to 'all program statements'.
    If 'flags count' is less than flags_min then, Set flags_min to 'flags count'.
    If 'flags count' is greater than flags_max then, Set flags_max to 'flags count'.
    If 'things count' is less than things_min then, Set things_min to 'things count'.
    If 'things count' is greater than things_max then, Set things_max to 'things count'.
    If 'functions count' is less than functions_min then, Set functions_min to 'functions count'.
    If 'functions count' is greater than functions_max then, Set functions_max to 'functions count'.
    If 'declarations count' is less than globals_min then, Set globals_min to 'declarations count'.
    If 'declarations count' is greater than globals_max then, Set globals_max to 'declarations count'.
    If 'buffers count' is less than buffers_min then, Set buffers_min to 'buffers count'.
    If 'buffers count' is greater than buffers_max then, Set buffers_max to 'buffers count'.
    If 'loops count' is less than loops_min then, Set loops_min to 'loops count'.
    If 'loops count' is greater than loops_max then, Set loops_max to 'loops count'.
    Set 'programs processed' to 'programs processed' add 1.

(find the first index of `target` in `the list`, or 0 if absent - used
 both to locate an invariant statement's position within one program's
 statement list, and by the sort below.)
To 'first index of' with a text called target and a list called 'the list'.
    a number called 'the list length' is 'the list's length.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a number called found is 0.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the list length' and found is 0,
        a text called 'candidate value' is element 'the scan position' of 'the list',
        If 'candidate value' is target then, Set found to 'the scan position'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    Return a number, found.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 main
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

a list called 'the args' is arguments's all.
a number called 'argument count' is 'the args's length.
If 'argument count' is less than 1 then,
    open a file for writing called 'the error stream' at 2,
    Write "usage: invariants <corpus-dir>\n" to 'the error stream',
    Close 'the error stream',
    Exit 2.

a text called 'the corpus dir' is element 1 of 'the args'.

(Vox has no readdir/glob - shell out to `find`, write the listing to a
 dotfile inside the corpus dir so it never matches *.vox itself, then
 read it back with the same 'Read line' loop used for the programs.)
a text called 'the list path' is "{'the corpus dir'}/.invariants-filelist.tmp".
a text called 'the quoted dir' is 'shell quote' of 'the corpus dir'.
a text called 'the quoted list path' is 'shell quote' of 'the list path'.
a text called 'the list command' is "find {'the quoted dir'} -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.vox' | sort > {'the quoted list path'}".
'run shell' of 'the list command'.

open a file for reading called listf at 'the list path'.
a buffer called 'list line'.
While true,
    Read line from listf into 'list line',
    If 'list line' is empty then, Break.
    a number called 'length before lf trim' is 'list line's size,
    a number called 'last byte before lf trim' is 0,
    If 'length before lf trim' is greater than 0 then, Set 'last byte before lf trim' to byte 'length before lf trim' of 'list line'.
    a boolean called 'ends lf2' is false,
    If 'last byte before lf trim' is 10 then, Set 'ends lf2' to true.
    If 'ends lf2' then, resize 'list line' to {'length before lf trim' subtract 1}.
    a number called 'length before cr trim' is 'list line's size,
    a number called 'last byte before cr trim' is 0,
    If 'length before cr trim' is greater than 0 then, Set 'last byte before cr trim' to byte 'length before cr trim' of 'list line'.
    a boolean called 'ends cr2' is false,
    If 'last byte before cr trim' is 13 then, Set 'ends cr2' to true.
    If 'ends cr2' then, resize 'list line' to {'length before cr trim' subtract 1}.
    If 'list line' is empty then, Continue.
    a buffer called 'one path copy' is 'list line',
    a text called 'one path' is 'one path copy' as text,
    append 'one path' to 'all program paths'.
Close listf.
'run shell' of "rm -f {'the quoted list path'}".

a number called 'total programs' is 'all program paths's length.
If 'total programs' is 0 then,
    Print "invariants: no .vox files found in {'the corpus dir'}",
    Exit 1.

(pass 1: read every program once, fill every accumulator)
For each path in 'all program paths', 'process program' of path.

Print "invariants: {'total programs'} programs in {'the corpus dir'}".

(--- construct counts: report only the categories fixed at min==max ---)
If flags_min is equal to flags_max then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "identical-count" to 'report categories', append "flags: always exactly {flags_min} flag declarations per program" to 'report texts'.
If things_min is equal to things_max then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "identical-count" to 'report categories', append "things: always exactly {things_min} thing declarations per program" to 'report texts'.
If functions_min is equal to functions_max then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "identical-count" to 'report categories', append "functions: always exactly {functions_min} function declarations per program" to 'report texts'.
If globals_min is equal to globals_max then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "identical-count" to 'report categories', append "declarations: always exactly {globals_min} variable declarations per program" to 'report texts'.
If buffers_min is equal to buffers_max then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "identical-count" to 'report categories', append "buffers: always exactly {buffers_min} buffer declarations per program" to 'report texts'.
If loops_min is equal to loops_max then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "identical-count" to 'report categories', append "loops: always exactly {loops_min} while/for-each loops per program" to 'report texts'.

(--- identical statements, and the 100%-present subset for the ordering pass ---)
a list called 'all statement keys' is program_count_of_statement's keys.
For each key in 'all statement keys',
    a number called 'program count' is program_count_of_statement's "{key}",
    a number called 'percent present' is {'program count' multiply 100} divide 'total programs',
    If 'percent present' is greater than or equal to 'suspicion threshold percent' then,
        a number called 'total occurrences' is total_count_of_statement's "{key}",
        append 'percent present' to 'report scores',
        append "identical-statement" to 'report categories',
        append "{'percent present'}% ({'program count'}/{'total programs'} programs, {'total occurrences'} occurrences): \"{key}\"" to 'report texts'.
    If 'program count' is equal to 'total programs' then, append key to 'invariant statement keys'.
    Continue.

(--- fixed vocabulary: identifiers spelled the same in every program ---)
a list called 'all ident keys' is program_count_of_ident's keys.
For each identifier in 'all ident keys',
    a number called 'program count' is program_count_of_ident's "{identifier}",
    a number called 'percent present' is {'program count' multiply 100} divide 'total programs',
    If 'percent present' is greater than or equal to 'suspicion threshold percent' then,
        append 'percent present' to 'report scores',
        append "fixed-vocabulary" to 'report categories',
        append "{'percent present'}% ({'program count'}/{'total programs'} programs) use the identifier `{identifier}`" to 'report texts'.
    Continue.

(--- never-exceeded bounds: recurring templates with >=1 digit slot ---)
a list called 'all skel keys' is skeleton_program_count's keys.
For each skeleton in 'all skel keys',
    a number called 'program count' is skeleton_program_count's "{skeleton}",
    a number called 'percent present' is {'program count' multiply 100} divide 'total programs',
    a boolean called 'skel meets threshold' is false,
    If 'percent present' is greater than or equal to 'suspicion threshold percent' then, Set 'skel meets threshold' to true.
    a number called 'slot count' is 0,
    If 'skel meets threshold' then, Set 'slot count' to skeleton_slot_count's "{skeleton}".
    (a nested While that is a sibling of the outer loop's body, not
     wrapped in the threshold If's then-branch - 'slot count' is
     already 0 when the threshold is not met, so this is a no-op then,
     matching the loop-at-its-own-level discipline used throughout)
    a number called slotj is 1,
    While slotj is less than or equal to 'slot count',
        a text called 'bound key' is "{skeleton}#{slotj}",
        a number called 'max value' is skeleton_max_slot's "{'bound key'}",
        append 'percent present' to 'report scores',
        append "never-exceeded-bound" to 'report categories',
        append "{'percent present'}% ({'program count'}/{'total programs'} programs) template \"{skeleton}\" slot {slotj}: never above {'max value'}" to 'report texts',
        Set slotj to slotj add 1.
    Continue.

(--- fixed ordering / contiguity over the 100%-present statement set ---)
a number called 'inv count' is 'invariant statement keys's length.
(there is always at least one program by this point - 'total programs'
 was checked above - so element 1 is always safe regardless of how many
 invariant lines there are)
a list called 'first program statements' is element 1 of 'all program statements'.
(canonical order candidate: position in program 1, ascending, via a
 selection sort that only ever reads by index and rebuilds by
 appending - Vox lists document append/read but not an in-place
 index-assignment for lists, so removal-by-rebuild avoids relying on
 one. Every If below sits at its own While's level - none nested inside
 another If's then-branch - so a period never closes more than the one
 clause it plainly ends. If 'invariant statement keys' has 0 or 1 entries the
 loop bounds below are never satisfied, so no special-casing is needed.)
a list called 'remaining keys' is 'invariant statement keys'.
a list called 'ordered keys' is [].
While 'remaining keys's length is greater than 0,
    a number called 'remaining count' is 'remaining keys's length,
    a text called 'first remaining key' is element 1 of 'remaining keys',
    a number called 'best line index' is 1,
    a number called 'best line position' is 'first index of' of 'first remaining key' and 'first program statements',
    a number called 'candidate index' is 2,
    While 'candidate index' is less than or equal to 'remaining count',
        a text called 'candidate key' is element 'candidate index' of 'remaining keys',
        a number called 'candidate position' is 'first index of' of 'candidate key' and 'first program statements',
        If 'candidate position' is less than 'best line position' then, Set 'best line position' to 'candidate position', Set 'best line index' to 'candidate index'.
        Set 'candidate index' to 'candidate index' add 1.
    a text called 'chosen key' is element 'best line index' of 'remaining keys',
    append 'chosen key' to 'ordered keys',
    a list called 'rebuilt keys' is [],
    a number called 'rebuild index' is 1,
    While 'rebuild index' is less than or equal to 'remaining count',
        a text called 'rebuild key' is element 'rebuild index' of 'remaining keys',
        If 'rebuild index' is not 'best line index' then, append 'rebuild key' to 'rebuilt keys'.
        Set 'rebuild index' to 'rebuild index' add 1.
    Set 'remaining keys' to 'rebuilt keys'.

(walk adjacent pairs in canonical order; classify each edge as
 contiguous / ordered-but-not-contiguous / broken, and merge runs of
 contiguous edges into one block finding each)
a number called blockstart is 1.
a number called pk is 1.
a number called 'ordering pass limit' is 'inv count' subtract 1.
While pk is less than or equal to 'ordering pass limit',
    a text called keyA is element pk of 'ordered keys',
    a number called pkplus1 is pk add 1,
    a text called keyB is element pkplus1 of 'ordered keys',
    a boolean called 'ordering holds' is true,
    a boolean called 'contiguous holds' is true,
    a number called pp is 1,
    While pp is less than or equal to 'total programs',
        a list called 'these statements' is element pp of 'all program statements',
        a number called posA is 'first index of' of keyA and 'these statements',
        a number called posB is 'first index of' of keyB and 'these statements',
        a number called posAplus1 is posA add 1,
        If posB is less than or equal to posA then, Set 'ordering holds' to false.
        If posB is not posAplus1 then, Set 'contiguous holds' to false.
        Set pp to pp add 1.
    a boolean called 'flush block' is false,
    If 'contiguous holds' is false and pk is greater than blockstart then, Set 'flush block' to true.
    a text called firstkey is element blockstart of 'ordered keys',
    a text called lastkey is element pk of 'ordered keys',
    a number called blocklenraw is pk subtract blockstart,
    a number called blocklen is blocklenraw add 1,
    If 'flush block' then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "fixed-ordering" to 'report categories', append "100% ({'total programs'}/{'total programs'} programs) block of {blocklen} statements always contiguous and in this order, from \"{firstkey}\" to \"{lastkey}\"" to 'report texts'.
    a boolean called 'emit single edge' is false,
    If 'contiguous holds' is false and 'ordering holds' then, Set 'emit single edge' to true.
    If 'emit single edge' then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "fixed-ordering" to 'report categories', append "100% ({'total programs'}/{'total programs'} programs) \"{keyA}\" always precedes \"{keyB}\" (gap varies)" to 'report texts'.
    If 'contiguous holds' is false then, Set blockstart to pkplus1.
    Set pk to pk add 1.

a boolean called 'flush final block' is false.
If 'inv count' is greater than blockstart then, Set 'flush final block' to true.
a text called firstkey2 is "".
a text called lastkey2 is "".
a number called blocklen2 is 0.
If 'flush final block' then, Set firstkey2 to element blockstart of 'ordered keys', Set lastkey2 to element 'inv count' of 'ordered keys', Set blocklen2 to 'inv count' subtract blockstart, Set blocklen2 to blocklen2 add 1.
If 'flush final block' then, append 100 to 'report scores', append "fixed-ordering" to 'report categories', append "100% ({'total programs'}/{'total programs'} programs) block of {blocklen2} statements always contiguous and in this order, from \"{firstkey2}\" to \"{lastkey2}\"" to 'report texts'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 sort all findings by suspicion (score desc) and print, capped at
 'max findings shown' with an aggregated tail count per category.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

Print "".
Print "suspicion  category              finding                                                        citation".

a list called 'remaining scores' is 'report scores'.
a list called 'remaining categories' is 'report categories'.
a list called 'remaining texts' is 'report texts'.
a number called 'total findings' is 'report scores's length.
a number called shown is 0.
a number called 'agg identical-statement' is 0.
a number called 'agg identical-count' is 0.
a number called 'agg fixed-vocabulary' is 0.
a number called 'agg never-exceeded-bound' is 0.
a number called 'agg fixed-ordering' is 0.

While 'remaining scores's length is greater than 0,
    a number called 'remaining count' is 'remaining scores's length,
    a number called 'best finding index' is 1,
    a number called 'best score' is element 1 of 'remaining scores',
    a number called 'candidate index' is 2,
    While 'candidate index' is less than or equal to 'remaining count',
        a number called 'candidate score' is element 'candidate index' of 'remaining scores',
        If 'candidate score' is greater than 'best score' then, Set 'best score' to 'candidate score', Set 'best finding index' to 'candidate index'.
        Set 'candidate index' to 'candidate index' add 1.
    a text called 'chosen category' is element 'best finding index' of 'remaining categories',
    a text called 'chosen text' is element 'best finding index' of 'remaining texts',
    a boolean called 'is capped' is false,
    If shown is greater than or equal to 'max findings shown' then, Set 'is capped' to true.
    If 'is capped' is false then, Print "{'best score'}%\t{'chosen category'}\t{'chosen text'}\t", Set shown to shown add 1.
    If 'is capped' and 'chosen category' is "identical-statement" then, Set 'agg identical-statement' to 'agg identical-statement' add 1.
    If 'is capped' and 'chosen category' is "identical-count" then, Set 'agg identical-count' to 'agg identical-count' add 1.
    If 'is capped' and 'chosen category' is "fixed-vocabulary" then, Set 'agg fixed-vocabulary' to 'agg fixed-vocabulary' add 1.
    If 'is capped' and 'chosen category' is "never-exceeded-bound" then, Set 'agg never-exceeded-bound' to 'agg never-exceeded-bound' add 1.
    If 'is capped' and 'chosen category' is "fixed-ordering" then, Set 'agg fixed-ordering' to 'agg fixed-ordering' add 1.
    a list called 'rebuilt scores' is [],
    a list called 'rebuilt categories' is [],
    a list called 'rebuilt texts' is [],
    a number called 'rebuild index' is 1,
    While 'rebuild index' is less than or equal to 'remaining count',
        a number called 'rebuild score' is element 'rebuild index' of 'remaining scores',
        a text called 'rebuild category' is element 'rebuild index' of 'remaining categories',
        a text called 'rebuild text' is element 'rebuild index' of 'remaining texts',
        If 'rebuild index' is not 'best finding index' then, append 'rebuild score' to 'rebuilt scores', append 'rebuild category' to 'rebuilt categories', append 'rebuild text' to 'rebuilt texts'.
        Set 'rebuild index' to 'rebuild index' add 1.
    Set 'remaining scores' to 'rebuilt scores',
    Set 'remaining categories' to 'rebuilt categories',
    Set 'remaining texts' to 'rebuilt texts'.

Print "".
Print "total findings: {'total findings'} (threshold {'suspicion threshold percent'}%, showing top {'max findings shown'})".
If 'total findings' is greater than 'max findings shown' then,
    Print "aggregated remainder below the display cap, by category:",
    Print "  identical-statement: {'agg identical-statement'}",
    Print "  identical-count: {'agg identical-count'}",
    Print "  fixed-vocabulary: {'agg fixed-vocabulary'}",
    Print "  never-exceeded-bound: {'agg never-exceeded-bound'}",
    Print "  fixed-ordering: {'agg fixed-ordering'}".

Exit 0.
